{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 40, :comments ["<b>And he who eats of its carcass</b> — On the level of peshat, it speaks of one who eats in the manner that people eat, who touches and carries what he is eating, and becomes impure through contact and through carrying. It was necessary to mention this lest one suppose that his impurity is greater on account of the eating. And it mentions this in the case of a clean animal because a person might err and eat it, thinking it has been properly slaughtered, and there is no way to err thus with the forbidden species. And the Torah's manner is to speak of the typical case. According to the midrash (Niddah 42a), it comes to give a measure for carrying and for touching in the manner of eating, and that is a ke-zayit. It also mentions this regarding the carcass of a clean animal, and the same applies to the unclean one mentioned above (verses 24–25), for it completes here the laws of the impurities. And according to the view of the midrash of Torat Kohanim (on verse 26), all the impurities of carcasses are derived from here, and the section above (verses 24–25) concerns limbs. I have already mentioned this matter (Ramban on Leviticus 11:22)."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:53:05.608648425Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}